Yoga Vāsiṣṭha – Brahma Gītā
Sarga 186 (Part 12)
Practice, Projection, and the Ever-Free Witness
This passage reveals one of the most profound insights of Advaita.
Vasiṣṭha explains that just as a city imagined in a dream appears completely real to the dreamer, this universe also appears real within the vast projection of consciousness. Reality, as we ordinarily experience it, is shaped by the force of deeply rooted conviction.
A purified sage sees through this projection because the mind has become transparent. An impure mind, however, remains bound by its own conditioning and habitual identifications.
The illustration is striking.
One who is deeply convinced that he is bound in iron chains carries that conviction even into dreams. Consciousness faithfully reflects whatever has been repeatedly impressed upon it.
This is why spiritual practice is essentially the transformation of our deepest habit of identification.
When the world is our constant habit, we experience limitation.
When the Self becomes our living recognition, every experience shines as Consciousness alone.
Yet beyond both habit and projection stands the changeless Truth.
The infinite Space of Awareness illumines the seer, the seen, and the act of seeing, while remaining untouched by all three.
Realization is not the acquisition of something new.
It is the recognition of the ever-present Witness that has silently illumined every experience from the very beginning.
Hari Om Tat Sat
